</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The moduli space of curves is rigid</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Paul Hacking
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that the moduli stack <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of stable curves of genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points is rigid, that is, has no infinitesimal deformations. This confirms the first case of a principle proposed by Kapranov. It can also be viewed as a version of Mostow rigidity for the mapping class group.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kapranov has proposed the following informal statement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kapranov97</a>]</cite>.
Given a smooth variety <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X(0)</annotation></semantics></math>,
consider the moduli space <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(1)</annotation></semantics></math> of varieties obtained as deformations of
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(2)</annotation></semantics></math> of deformations of <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on.
Then this
process should stop after <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math> steps, i.e., <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(n)</annotation></semantics></math> should be rigid (no
infinitesimal deformations).
Roughly speaking, one thinks of <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(1)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of a sheaf of non-abelian groups on <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, at least the tangent space to <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(1)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the tangent sheaf, the sheaf of first order infinitesimal automorphisms of <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then one regards <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(m)</annotation></semantics></math> as a kind of non-Abelian <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the analogy with the usual
definition of Abelian <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> suggests the statement above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the moduli space of curves should be rigid. In this paper, we
verify this in the following precise form: the moduli stack of stable
curves of genus <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points is rigid for each <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, moduli spaces of surfaces should have non-trivial
deformations in general. A simple example (for surfaces with boundary) is given in
Sec. <a href="#S6" title="6 Nonrigidity of moduli of surfaces ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. It seems plausible that there should be a non-trivial deformation of
a moduli space of surfaces whose fibres parametrise “generalised surfaces” in some sense,
for example non-commutative surfaces. From this point of view the result of this paper
says that the concept of a curve cannot be deformed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let us also note that our result can be thought of as a version of Mostow rigidity for the mapping class group.
Recall that the moduli space <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth complex curves of genus <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the
quotient of the Teichmüller space <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by the mapping class group <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bounded domain in <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{3g-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{3g-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is homeomorphic to a ball,
and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> acts discontinuously on <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite stabilisers.
We thus obtain <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a complex orbifold with orbifold fundamental group <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a natural metric, the Weil–Petersson metric, which has negative
holomorphic sectional curvatures. So, roughly speaking, <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> looks like a quotient of
a complex ball by a discrete group <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of isometries, with finite volume. Mostow rigidity
predicts that such a quotient is uniquely determined by the group <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> up to complex conjugation.
(This is certainly true if <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely with compact quotient, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siu80</a>]</cite>.)
In particular, it should have no infinitesimal deformations. Unfortunately I do not know a proof along these lines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Acknowledgements</em>: We thank M. Kapranov for suggesting the problem and subsequent encouragement.
We also thank I. Dolgachev, G. Farkas, S. Grushevsky, S. Keel, J. Kollár, S. Kovács, R. Lazarsfeld, A. Marian, M. Olsson,
S.P. Smith and T. Stafford for helpful discussions. The author was partially supported by NSF grant DMS-0650052.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Statements</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work over an algebraically closed field <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic zero.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be non-negative integers such that <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the moduli stack of stable curves of genus <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points.
The stack <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth proper Deligne–Mumford stack of dimension <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The stack <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid, that is, has no infinitesimal deformations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the boundary of the moduli stack,
that is, the complement of the locus of smooth curves (with its reduced structure).
The locus <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing divisor in <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pair <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> has no locally trivial deformations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the coarse moduli space of the stack <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective variety with quotient singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The variety <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has no locally trivial deformations if <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,2),(2,0),(2,1),(3,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,2),(2,0),(2,1),(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the exceptional cases, the projection <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is
ramified in codimension one over the interior of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and an additional calculation is needed to relate the deformations of
the stack and the deformations of the coarse moduli space (cf. Prop. <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 5.2. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).
Presumably the result still holds.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Write <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> for the boundary of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(\log\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(\log\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sheaf of <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with logarithmic poles along the boundary,
and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> the dual of <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(\log\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(\log\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sheaf <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> is the subsheaf of the tangent sheaf <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of vector fields on <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
which are tangent to the boundary.
In other words, it is the sheaf of first order infinitesimal automorphisms of the pair <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence the first order locally trivial deformations of the pair <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> are identified with the space
<math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))</annotation></semantics></math>. To prove Thm. <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we show <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the universal family over <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-pointed stable curves of genus <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> together with an extra section (with no smoothness condition).
Let <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denote the union of the <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tautological sections of <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the boundary <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the union of <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\colon\mathcal{B}^{\nu}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\colon\mathcal{B}^{\nu}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normalisation of the boundary <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the normal bundle of the map <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\nu}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\nu}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we have an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the relative dualising sheaf of the morphism <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a natural isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta:T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\sim}}{{\longrightarrow}}R^{1}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta:T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\sim}}{{\longrightarrow}}R^{1}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a pointed stable curve <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\Sigma_{C}=x_{1}+\cdots+x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\Sigma_{C}=x_{1}+\cdots+x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of first order deformations is equal to <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ext^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ext^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM69</a>, p.79–82]</cite>. The surjection</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ext^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C}))=\bigoplus_{q\in\Sing C}\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})_{q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Sing</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ext^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C}))=\bigoplus_{q\in\Sing C}\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">sends a global deformation of <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\Sigma_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\Sigma_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> to the induced deformations of the nodes.
Étale locally at the point <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(C,\Sigma_{C})]\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(C,\Sigma_{C})]\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the boundary <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing divisor with components <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by the nodes <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (the divisor <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the locus where the node <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> smoothed).
The Kodaira–Spencer map identifies the fibre of the normal bundle of <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(C,\Sigma_{C})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(C,\Sigma_{C})]</annotation></semantics></math>
with the stalk of <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now work globally over <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We omit the subscripts <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> for clarity. Consider the exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\pi^{*}\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\Omega_{\mathcal{U}}(\log\Sigma)\rightarrow\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\pi^{*}\Omega_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\Omega_{\mathcal{U}}(\log\Sigma)\rightarrow\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">For a sheaf <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cExt^{i}_{\pi}(\mathcal{F},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cExt^{i}_{\pi}(\mathcal{F},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th right derived functor of <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\circ\cHom(\mathcal{F},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\circ\cHom(\mathcal{F},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\circ\cHom(\cdot,\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\circ\cHom(\cdot,\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math> to the exact sequence <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ref{Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ref{Omega})</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a long exact sequence with connecting homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kodaira–Spencer map for the universal family over <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus is an isomorphism.
(Note that, for a point <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=[(C,\Sigma_{C})]\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=[(C,\Sigma_{C})]\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the base change map</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\otimes k(p)\rightarrow\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\otimes k(p)\rightarrow\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{C}(\Sigma_{C}),\mathcal{O}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">is an isomorphism. Indeed, by relative duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kleiman80</a>, Thm. 21]</cite>, it suffices to show that <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)\otimes\omega_{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)\otimes\omega_{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>
commutes with base change. This follows from cohomology and base change.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the two exact sequences</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow R^{1}\pi_{*}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)^{\vee})\rightarrow\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\rightarrow\pi_{*}\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow R^{1}\pi_{*}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)^{\vee})\rightarrow\cExt^{1}_{\pi}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\rightarrow\pi_{*}\cExt^{1}(\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma),\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{U}})\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">The Kodaira–Spencer map <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> identifies the middle terms, and induces an identification of the right end terms determined by the deformations of the singularities of the fibres of <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
We thus obtain a natural isomorphism <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> of the left end terms.
Finally, note that <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)^{\vee}=\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)^{\vee}=\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible and agrees with
<math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\mathcal{U}/\overline{\mathcal{M}}}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> in codimension <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The line bundle <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is ample on fibres of <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> by dimensions.
So <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i+1}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=H^{i}(R^{1}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i+1}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=H^{i}(R^{1}\pi_{*}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee}))</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by the Leray spectral sequence.
Hence the isomorphism <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\sim}}{{\longrightarrow}}H^{i+1}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\sim}}{{\longrightarrow}}H^{i+1}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the coarse moduli space of the stack <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow U_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow U_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> the projection.
The line bundle <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> on the stack <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>
on the coarse moduli space <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Sec. <a href="#S7" title="7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
We use the following important result, which is essentially due to Arakelov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arakelov71</a>, Prop. 3.2, p. 1297]</cite>.
We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keel99</a>, Sec. 4]</cite> for the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is big and nef on <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It follows by Kodaira vanishing (see Thm <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>) that
<math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining with (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we deduce</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining cases are easy to check.
This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove that <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid.
Since <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack, its first order infinitesimal deformations are identified with the space <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and we must show that <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the exact sequence</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B})\rightarrow T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}\rightarrow\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the associated long exact sequence of cohomology</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdots\rightarrow H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))\rightarrow H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H^{i}(\mathcal{N})\rightarrow\cdots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdots\rightarrow H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))\rightarrow H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H^{i}(\mathcal{N})\rightarrow\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We prove below that <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{N})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{N})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}}(-\log\mathcal{B}))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by Prop. <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
so we deduce</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the remaining cases it is easy to check that <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{N})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{N})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so again <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The irreducible components of the normalisation <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite images of the following stacks
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knudsen83a</a>, Def. 3.8, Cor. 3.9]</cite>:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},S_{1}\cup\{n+1\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},S_{2}\cup\{n+2\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},S_{1}\cup\{n+1\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},S_{2}\cup\{n+2\}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}+g_{2}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}+g_{2}=g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a partition of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the moduli stack of stable curves of genus <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> with marked points labelled by a finite set <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
In each case the map to <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by identifying the points labelled by <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The map is an isomorphism onto the component of <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> except in case (1) for <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=g_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=g_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and case (2),
when it is étale of degree <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}</annotation></semantics></math> a moduli stack of pointed stable curves as above, let <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\mathcal{U}_{h,S}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\mathcal{U}_{h,S}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote
the universal family, and <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{h,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{h,S}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{h,S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, the tautological sections of <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}=x_{i}^{*}\omega_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}=x_{i}^{*}\omega_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback of the relative dualising sheaf of <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> along the section <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result is well-known, see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">HMo98</a>, Prop. 3.32]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pullback of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},S_{1}\cup\{n+1\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},S_{2}\cup\{n+2\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},S_{1}\cup\{n+1\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},S_{2}\cup\{n+2\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pr_{1}^{*}\psi_{n+1}\otimes\pr_{2}^{*}\psi_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pr_{1}^{*}\psi_{n+1}\otimes\pr_{2}^{*}\psi_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similiarly, the pullback of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n+1}\otimes\psi_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n+1}\otimes\psi_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">There is an isomorphism of stacks <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
which identifies the morphism <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n+1}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n+1}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
given by forgetting the last point with the projection <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\mathcal{U}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knudsen83a</a>, Sec. 1–2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knudsen83b</a><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Thm. 4.1(d), p. 202]</span></cite><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
The line bundle <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the pullback of the line bundle <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>
under the isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle on the coarse moduli space of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is big and nef on each component</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows immediately from Lem. <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, Lem. <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, and Thm. <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We deduce that <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{N})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{N})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> by Thm <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>.
This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 2.3. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first prove a basic result which relates the deformations of a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack and its coarse moduli space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth proper Deligne–Mumford stack, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> the coarse moduli space of <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> the projection.
Let <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the tangent sheaf of <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of the codimension one components of the branch locus of <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> (with its reduced structure).
Let <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}(-\log D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}(-\log D)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subsheaf of the tangent sheaf <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of derivations which preserve the ideal sheaf of <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is the sheaf of first order infinitesimal automorphisms of the pair <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}=T_{X}(-\log D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}=T_{X}(-\log D)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The sheaves <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}(-\log D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}(-\log D)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy Serre’s <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> condition, and are identified over the locus where <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is étale.
So it suffices to work in codimension <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We reduce to the case <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{e}\ni\zeta:x\mapsto\zeta x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∋</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{e}\ni\zeta:x\mapsto\zeta x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x^{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(y=0)\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(y=0)\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}\rightarrow\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}\rightarrow\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> be the quotient map.
We compute</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}=\left(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\right)^{\mu_{e}}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}}\cdot x\frac{\partial}{\partial x}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}}\cdot y\frac{\partial}{\partial y}=T_{X}(-\log D)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}}=\left(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\right)^{\mu_{e}}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}}\cdot x\frac{\partial}{\partial x}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{y}}\cdot y\frac{\partial}{\partial y}=T_{X}(-\log D),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The first order deformations of the stack <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified with the first order locally trivial deformations of the pair <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the Lemma, <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\mathcal{X}})=H^{1}(p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}})=H^{1}(T_{X}(-\log D))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\mathcal{X}})=H^{1}(p_{*}T_{\mathcal{X}})=H^{1}(T_{X}(-\log D))</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now apply this result to relate deformations of the stack <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and its coarse moduli space <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A stable <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-pointed curve of genus <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> has no non-trivial automorphisms.
Hence the stack <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to its coarse moduli space <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid by Thm. <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
Also, recall that <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore rigid.
So, in the following, we assume that <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the component of the boundary whose general point is a curve with two components of genus <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math>
meeting in a node, with each of the <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points on the component of genus <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that each point of <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> has a non-trivial automorphism given by the involution of the component of genus <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> fixing the node.
Let <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection, and <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> the coarse moduli space of <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">HMu82</a><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, §2]</span></cite><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> then the automorphism group of a general point of <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial,
and the divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique codimension <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> component of the branch locus of <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:\mathcal{D}^{\nu}\rightarrow\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:\mathcal{D}^{\nu}\rightarrow\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normalisation of <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\nu}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\nu}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normal bundle of the map <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\nu}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\nu}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}(-\log D)\rightarrow T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}\rightarrow p_{*}\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\otimes 2}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}(-\log D)\rightarrow T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}\rightarrow p_{*}\nu_{*}\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\otimes 2}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is a straightforward calculation similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">HMu82</a>, Lemma, p. 52]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We have <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}(-\log D))=H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}}(-\log D))=H^{1}(T_{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Prop. <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 5.2. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and Thm. <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
Also <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\otimes 2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\otimes 2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Thm. <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> because the <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle
defined by <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{D}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> on the coarse moduli space of <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is big and nef by Cor. <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 4.4. ‣ 4 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
So <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\overline{M}_{g,n}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Lem. <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 5.4. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, that is, <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has no locally trivial deformations.
This concludes the proof of Thm. <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 2.3. ‣ 2 Statements ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Nonrigidity of moduli of surfaces</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We exhibit a moduli space of surfaces with boundary that is not rigid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\ldots,P_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\ldots,P_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> points in linear general position in <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> be the line through <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> be a line through the point <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=l_{12}\cap l_{34}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>34</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=l_{12}\cap l_{34}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> does not pass
through <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{13}\cap l_{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>24</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{13}\cap l_{24}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{14}\cap l_{23}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{14}\cap l_{23}</annotation></semantics></math> and is not equal to <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{34}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>34</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{34}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the blowup of the points <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\ldots,P_{4},Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\ldots,P_{4},Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> the sum of
the strict transforms of <math id="S6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and the exceptional curves.
Then <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,B)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth surface with normal crossing boundary such that <math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{S}+B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{S}+B</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample.
We fix an ordering <math id="S6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> of the components of <math id="S6.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
The moduli stack <math id="S6.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> of deformations of <math id="S6.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,B)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S6.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{4}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="S6.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct points. Indeed, it suffices to observe that all deformations of
<math id="S6.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,B)</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by the construction above.
The moduli space <math id="S6.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has a modular compactification <math id="S6.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Kollár–Shepherd-Barron–Alexeev moduli stack of stable surfaces with boundary, which is isomorphic
to <math id="S6.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}^{1},\sum q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}^{1},\sum q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, the pair <math id="S6.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}},\partial\overline{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> has non-trivial deformations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The compact moduli space <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an instance of the compactifications of moduli spaces of hyperplane arrangements described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lafforgue03</a>]</cite> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">HKT06</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth proper Deligne–Mumford stack, <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> the coarse moduli space of <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> the projection.
Étale locally on <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:[U/G]\rightarrow U/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:[U/G]\rightarrow U/G</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth affine variety and <math id="S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite group acting on <math id="S7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AV02</a>, Lemma 2.2.3, p. 32]</cite>.
A sheaf <math id="S7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[U/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[U/G]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a <math id="S7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant sheaf <math id="S7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}\mathcal{F}=(\pi_{*}\mathcal{F}_{U})^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}\mathcal{F}=(\pi_{*}\mathcal{F}_{U})^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:U\rightarrow U/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:U\rightarrow U/G</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a line bundle on <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be sufficiently divisible so that for each open patch <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[U/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[U/G]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
as above and point <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in U</annotation></semantics></math> the stabilizer <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on the fibre of <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{U}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>U</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{U}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the pushforward <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define <math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{n}p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})\in\Pic(X)\otimes\mathbb{Q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{n}p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})\in\Pic(X)\otimes\mathbb{Q},</annotation></semantics></math> and call <math id="S7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
the <math id="S7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle on <math id="S7.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S7.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that the coarse moduli space <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic variety.
If the <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{Q}}_{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is big and nef then <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the coarse moduli space <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth then Thm. <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO05</a>, Thm. 2.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> is proved by reducing to the following generalisation of the Kodaira vanishing theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM98</a><span id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Thm. 2.70, p. 73]</span></cite><span id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
Let <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper normal variety and <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-divisor on <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that the pair <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is Kawamata log terminal (klt).
Let <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Cartier Weil divisor on <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\equiv M+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\equiv M+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a big and nef <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Cartier <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-divisor.
Then <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Thm. <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe first that <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal variety with quotient singularities.
Consider the sheaf <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the automorphism group of a general point of <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> acts nontrivially on <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose now that the automorphism group of a general point acts trivially on <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank 1 reflexive sheaf on <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}=\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee}=\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a Weil divisor on <math id="S7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be sufficiently divisible so that <math id="S7.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(\mathcal{L})=\frac{1}{n}p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(\mathcal{L})=\frac{1}{n}p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> as above.
Let <math id="S7.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S7.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-divisor corresponding to the <math id="S7.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-line bundle <math id="S7.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a natural map <math id="S7.p4.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})^{\otimes n}\rightarrow p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∨</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})^{\otimes n}\rightarrow p_{*}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a map
<math id="S7.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(-nN)\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}(-nM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(-nN)\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}(-nM)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an isomorphism over the locus where <math id="S7.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is étale.
So <math id="S7.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\equiv M+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\equiv M+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective <math id="S7.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-divisor supported on the branch locus of <math id="S7.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\ldots,D_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\ldots,D_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the codimension <math id="S7.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> components of the branch locus.
Let <math id="S7.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ramification index at <math id="S7.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the age of the line bundle <math id="S7.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S7.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, after removing the automorphism group of a general point of <math id="S7.p4.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, a transverse slice of <math id="S7.p4.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> at a general point of <math id="S7.p4.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
is of the form <math id="S7.p4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e_{i}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{x}/\mu_{e_{i}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{e_{i}}\ni\zeta:x\mapsto\zeta\cdot x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∋</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{e_{i}}\ni\zeta:x\mapsto\zeta\cdot x</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S7.p4.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{e_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{e_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the fibre of <math id="S7.p4.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> by the character <math id="S7.p4.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\mapsto\zeta^{-a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\mapsto\zeta^{-a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p4.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a_{i}\leq e_{i}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a_{i}\leq e_{i}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute that <math id="S7.p4.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\sum\frac{a_{i}}{e_{i}}D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\sum\frac{a_{i}}{e_{i}}D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is klt.
Let <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}=\sum\frac{e_{i}-1}{e_{i}}D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}=\sum\frac{e_{i}-1}{e_{i}}D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathcal{X}}=p^{*}(K_{X}+\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathcal{X}}=p^{*}(K_{X}+\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, so <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is klt by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM98</a>, Prop. 5.20(4), p. 160]</cite>. Now <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\leq\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\leq\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-factorial, so <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is also klt.
We deduce that <math id="S7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(p_{*}\mathcal{L}^{\vee})=H^{i}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\dim\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\dim\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> by Thm. <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 7.3. ‣ 7 Appendix: Kodaira vanishing for stacks ‣ The moduli space of curves is rigid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
